\b\f5\cf4 Ten: Raid
\par }\pard \s16\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Retribution had come swift and hard for the actions of Jacque Barre, "Eden Spy."  Under cover of darkness, a Plymouth convoy had sliced through Eden's defenses, ignored choice strategic targets, and made a lightning raid on the labs researching starship t
echnology.  An attempt to remove salvage from the original starship, }{\i\f5\cf4 Conestoga}{\f5\cf4 
, had been repelled thanks to courageous action by the Volunteer Guard, but three scientists were missing and presumed captured.  Among these was Eldon Jensen.
\par 
\par Axen especially regretted Jensen\rquote 
s loss.  Failure was always easier to take when it didn't have a face.  Of course there was nothing he could do, once Barre caused the talks to collapse.  Emma, or at least the people she spoke for, simply didn't trust any
thing Eden had to say, anything they had to offer.  When the communications channel went dead one afternoon after a particularly heated session, it was no surprise.
\par 
\par The raid nine hours later was.
\par 
\par As Axen sat in front of one of Residence Three's gallery windows and stared out at the large hole blasted in the lab's side, still unrepaired, he hoped Plymouth had gotten something useful from the three captives, because the pendulum was swinging Plymout
h's way again, and it was bound to hit hard.
\par 
\par Alarmed by the ease with which the raid had penetrated their defenses, Eden had been given a new priority, an
d its splintered political factions had aligned behind one thing and one thing only: a massive military buildup.  They would have their scientists back, and the Gene Bank too.
\par 
\par Axen slumped, chin propped in his hand.  If this kept up, neither side would win.  Too many resources would be squandered on weapons, too much destroyed in battle.  Neither starship would be built, and humanity would die.  He had to keep the lid on this w
ar, keep things from falling completely apart before the most vital work for survival had been completed.
\par 
\par "Elder, you look like someone shot your dog."
\par 
\par He looked up as Zek Autzen stopped in front of him and handed him a ClipCom.  "What does that mean, the dog thing?"
\par 
\par "I don't know.  Saw it in an old vid once, and you had the same look on your face as the guy in the vid.  They never did show what a dog was, though."
\par 
\par Axen tapped the ClipCom with his finger.  "You read it?"
\par 
\par Zek nodded.  "Me and the chairman.  We agree with you that it's premature to take it to the whole Senate.  It's shocking, if true."
\par 
\par "It's true all right.  I'd stake my life on it."
\par 
\par Zek sighed.  "Even if it is, Elder, I don't know what we can do about it.  This could tear the Senate apart if we reveal it to them, destroy the uneasy truce we currently operate under.  The Masters would deny everything, everyone would choose up sides, a
nd meanwhile, it could force the Masters to play out their hand."
\par 
\par "You'll have proof."
\par 
\par "That some units, parts, and supplies may be unaccounted for?  That some people associated with 
the Masters didn't come back from the mining camp?  That one man is missing?  You can make a lot with that, but not a case.  If what you tell me is true, we need a }{\i\f5\cf4 location.}{\f5\cf4 
  So far, the satellite has turned up nothing, and even getting the resources to look is politically very difficult right now."
\par 
\par "They'd hardly leave it all lying around in plain sight.  They don't want either us or Plymouth to find it."
\par 
\par "I need more, Elder.  Give me something to work with and we can at least fight it out in the Senate.
"  He stood and started to walk away.  Then he stopped and half-turned back.  "Assuming you're right about what they've got, and what they're going to do with it, do you think they have a chance?"
\par 
\par Axen shrugged.  "It\rquote s a lot easier to destroy something than to steal it.  Even if they fail, they'll stir up Plymouth so bad that we'll have no hope of ever getting the Gene Bank, by }{\i\f5\cf4 any}{\f5\cf4  means.  Either way, they win."
\par 
\par Zek sighed.  "And humanity loses."
\par 
